        "signature": "",
        "thinking": "Good results:\n1. `GET /api/companies/:id/agents` - returns 200 with 0 agents (company just set up, no agents added)\n2. `GET /api/companies/9a7cf1f3...` - returns 500 with \"Cannot perform I/O on behalf of a different request\" — this is a CF Workers restriction that happens when we try to use I/O objects across request boundaries. \n3. `POST /api/auth/login` - returns 501 (not implemented in CF mode — auth routes are DB-only, might need to check)\n\n\n4. The root cause is that the postgres connection created during `buildRouteCache` is stream-based, and CF Workers doesn't allow reusing streams across different request handlers — each request gets its own isolated context, so the second request can't access the connection stream from the first one.\n\nThe solution is to either stop caching the connection entirely, or create a fresh connection for each request using the connection string from the environment. Looking at how `getRouteCache` works, it's checking if the connection string changed before rebuilding, but the real issue is that the connection object itself (which is a stream) can't be shared across requests in CF Workers' execution model. the DB connection (a postgres client with a TCP socket) is created once and cached. When the second request comes in and tries to use the cached connection, CF Workers throws \"Cannot perform I/O on behalf of a different request\" because the TCP socket was created in the context of request 1.\n\nThe fix: either:\n1. Create a new DB connection per request\n2. Or use a connection pool that's managed differently\n\nActually, looking at how Hyperdrive works: `env.HYPERDRIVE.connectionString` changes per request (it gives a connection-specific URL). So `routeCache.hyperdriveConnStr !== env.HYPERDRIVE.connectionString` would be true for each new request, forcing a new DB connection for each request.\n\nWait, but is `env.HYPERDRIVE.connectionString` changing per request? Let me think about this...\n\nIn production CF Workers, Hyperdrive provides a connection string that may be the same for the lifetime of an isolate but is re-created per worker invocation. The `routeCache` comparison checks if the connection string changed — but if it changes each request, the cache would be rebuilt each time. Locally in Miniflare, Hyperdrive always returns the same dummy value, so `routeCache` gets reused across requests. The real problem is that the postgres client creates a connection pool with TCP sockets tied to the first request's context, and CF Workers won't let subsequent requests use those same sockets.\n\nThe proper fix for CF Workers with Hyperdrive is to create a new postgres client per request, since Hyperdrive handles connection pooling on Cloudflare's side. I need to move the DB creation out of the route cache and create it fresh for each request, while keeping the routes/compiled routes list cached since those don't depend on the DB connection.",
        "type": "thinking"
      }
